(tied) of 127 since 1893.","t_rec":"Record highs keep coming. Record lows have nearly stopped.","p_rec":"If San Jose's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, San Jose has set <strong class=\"sig\">6 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 76 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">122 record highs</strong>, far more than the 76 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (45). San Jose's all-time high is 109\u00b0F, on September 6, 2022.","t_chg":"San Jose's days and nights both warmed. Nights warmed faster.","p_chg":"Across 119 complete years: San Jose's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.57\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.51 to +0.64), about +7.6\u00b0F since 1893. San Jose's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.20\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.15 to +0.25), about +2.6\u00b0F since 1893.","t_mon":"Lows rose in all 12 months. Highs rose in 9 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"San Jose's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (\u22120.10 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 13.7 inches a year. The wettest year was 1983 (32.57 in); the driest, 2013 (3.80 in). San Jose almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 6 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}